		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4009771)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4009771">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drmagnus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(318209); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=318209&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608151691" alt="DrMagnus" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=318209)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drmagnus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(318209); return false;" >DrMagnus</a></span> <span class="odate time_1538403039 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Oct 2018 14:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4009771">
									

<p>This has some of the same issues I see in most of the poetry on the site.</p>
<p>Your rhyme scheme is a bit all over the place, which doesn't really lend much to the actual work. If I'm seeing this correctly its:<br />
A bb a<br />
c dd c<br />
ef g ef<br />
h<br />
ijji<br />
kllk<br />
mn g mn<br />
h (non-exact match line-for-line but exact wording)<br />
o</p>
<p>I definitely like the scheme working cross-stanzas, but I would suggest either unifying the scheme to efgfe or mngnm for parity's sake.</p>
<p>I will say also that the lack of significant meter made this more difficult to appreciate the ryhme scheme, as I was too busy trying to keep in the flow of the wording which was broken up by a lot of double-accents and odd choices of singulars (such as &quot;And a jury&quot; which jogs you hard out of the flow of the previous line).</p>
<p>Theme wise, this hits the mark in a few places and not others. As a piece of poetry this makes sense, but I can't see it being the actual foundation oath in real life. Some of the wordings feel contrived in a not-pleasing way. Poets always screw with wording to make it fit, but in the cases I found, it wasn't serving the meter (which is what I generally mess with wording to accomplish).</p>
<p>Overall, I'm novoting for the above reasons. Poetry obviously doesn't <strong>need</strong> formal structures but if you use them, it gets distracting when it becomes harder to read. You've got a good rhyming structure, but the lack of consistent meter makes it hard to get in to the overall flow.</p>

							</div>

								
					
